Hi,

I'm intrigued by an off-list comment Anders made. I'm setting priors 
because I know that certain variables are constrained in a particular 
way, e.g. I treat a variable coded as 0-10 as continuous, so if Amelia 
draws an 11.3 that doesn't seem to be a reasonable value.

But are these values --mathematically speaking-- better to use if that's 
what the imputation spits out? My understanding is that whether I set a 
range or distribution prior, internally Amelia just draws from a normal 
distribution with specific s.d. and mean anyway rather than from a 
closed interval. Please correct me if I'm wrong about this. I would 
rather set distribution priors because I seem to be getting the

Error in chol(copy.theta[c(FALSE, m[ss, ]), c(FALSE, m[ss, ])]) :
	the leading minor of order 5 is not positive definite


every once in a while with range priors, but not with distribution priors.

Cheers,

Mark

